                        'biography' => "Al-Hasan ibn Ali, grandson and 'sweet basil' of the Prophet ﷺ, was the eldest son of Ali and Fatimah az-Zahra, and is counted by many scholars as the fifth rightly-guided caliph. Born in Medina in mid-Ramadan 3 AH, he was named al-Hasan by the Prophet himself, who performed his aqiqah; from face to navel he was the person most resembling the Messenger of Allah. The Prophet carried him on his shoulder praying, 'O Allah, I love him, so love him,' declared him and his brother 'the masters of the youth of Paradise', and from the pulpit pronounced his immortal prophecy: 'This son of mine is a master, and perhaps Allah will reconcile through him two great factions of the Muslims.' He preserved hadiths from his grandfather, including 'Leave what makes you doubt for what does not make you doubt.' Pledged the caliphate in Kufa after his father's martyrdom in 40 AH, he ruled about seven months, then — preferring to spare Muslim blood — ceded the caliphate to Mu'awiyah in 41 AH, the 'Year of Unity', fulfilling his grandfather's prophecy to the letter. He returned to Medina a worshipper of legendary generosity and forbearance, twice dividing his entire wealth with the poor and performing twenty-five pilgrimages on foot. He died in Medina in 49 AH, most reports say by poison, and was buried in al-Baqi near his mother Fatimah.",
